# House sparrow

The house sparrow is the small brown-and-grey sparrow that follows human
settlement almost everywhere on earth, feeding on seed and scraps in noisy
flocks and raising its young largely on insects. The male has a dark bib whose
size signals his standing in the flock, and both sexes dust-bathe communally in
dry earth. It builds a domed nest of grass crammed into a hole in a wall or
under a roof tile, wedged into the cavity rather than suspended from anything.
See [[passeridae]].
